WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:09.800
People come, people go...

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:10.800
It’s a fact of life.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.700
Dear friends, there are few places in the
world in which you can appreciate the games,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:26.910
the power plays, the rivalry between nations
as well as you can in the Middle East.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:31.700
Without a doubt, this is a region that has
become one of the great chess boards of international

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:32.700
politics.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:33.700
And...

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.500
It makes sense when you think about it, they
have all the essential elements:

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.050
The Middle East is located at the crossroads
of three distinct continents;

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.910
It’s the largest source of hydrocarbons
in the world;

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.770
It’s an enclave through which a good deal
of international trade passes,

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:50.100
and it’s the heartland of one of the biggest
religions on the planet.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.079
Not to mention that it’s also a huge piggy
bank.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:58.920
Energy, commerce, money, religion...

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.440
The Middle East has really become an international
player in recent decades.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.470
And, to top it off, we’re talking about
a place that has traditionally been a hornet's

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:07.470
nest.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:12.100
And, in recent years, this penchant for drama
has only increased.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:17.020
The truth is that today, you would be hard-pressed
to find a more conflictive place in the world.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.460
And no, I’m not exaggerating.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.690
Take a look at the historic conflict between
Israel and Palestine, in recent years we could

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:27.490
add the war in Syria and the war in Yemen
to the list.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:31.799
And let’s not forget the chaos that the
Islamic State caused when its leader Abu Bakr

00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:38.220
Al Baghdadi declared the restoration of the
Caliphate and invaded much of Iraq and Syria.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.360
But it doesn't end there.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.380
In addition, the Gulf Cooperation Council
has been fractured by the growing conflict

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.280
between Qatar against the United Arab Emirates
and Saudi Arabia.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:51.110
A conflict that has ended in the rupture of
almost all relations between the countries

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:52.110
and a savage blockade.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:56.869
Then, there’s the Iran cold war, Iraqi Kurdistan
trying to proclaim its self-determination

00:01:56.869 --> 00:02:00.560
and Erdogan's Turkey facing off against NATO
and invading Syria.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.020
In other words, there are conflicts galore.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.900
Of all sizes and colors.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.840
If you want a fight, you know where to find
it.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.950
However, all this chaos is hiding a very important
change, which has slowly begun in the Middle

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:12.970
East.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:19.620
You see, in recent decades the great American
Empire had a very strong hand in controlling

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:24.090
the region, but now, silently, and without
attracting attention, things are beginning

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.090
to shift.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:27.319
And all because another empire is on the scene.

00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:31.650
Now both empires are fighting for control
of the Middle East using very different strategies.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.599
Listen up.

00:02:35.599 --> 00:02:41.880
(THE ARRIVAL OF THE EASTERN EMPIRE)

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:48.000
In 2016, the Chinese government published
the “Arab Policy Paper”, its first political

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.690
strategy document for the Middle East.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:54.709
Here on VISUALPOLITIK we’ve been telling
you for a long time, how the Chinese government

00:02:54.709 --> 00:02:58.510
is slowly unfurling its tentacles across the
entire planet.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:03.510
But we’ve also told you that the growth
of the Chinese economy is slowing down, which

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.080
has forced China to curb its investments abroad.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.870
This has happened everywhere in the world
except for one place: The Middle East.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.849
And there’s a very good reason for this.

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:17.129
Right here on YOUTUBE, you can find very interesting
documentaries, such as how Nazi Germany was

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:21.930
forced to invest huge amounts of resources
to produce synthetic oil from coal.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.090
And right there was Germany’s Achilles heel:
the lack of fuel.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:28.070
Without energy there can be no power or prosperity.

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:33.790
And in a way, that's a modern problem too.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:37.489
China is the second biggest consumer of oil,
just behind the United States.

00:03:37.489 --> 00:03:41.420
The difference is that China, unlike the US
– or even Russia – has increasingly had

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:45.129
to import more and more oil to meet its needs.

00:03:45.129 --> 00:03:46.250
(CHART)

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:56.720
To give you an idea, today, half of all the
oil that China consumes comes from the Middle

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.860
East.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:03.000
(CHART)

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.020
And it's not just oil.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.819
The exact same thing happens with natural
gas.

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:11.670
In 2019, China will become the world's largest
natural gas importer across the globe.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:14.970
China accounts for a third of the world's
natural gas demand.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.580
And things are expected to become even worse
in the future.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:21.440
You see, the Chinese economy is an economy
that has been built on coal.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:22.770
Want to hear a curious fact?

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:31.970
Since 2011, China consumes more coal than
all other countries in the world put together.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:36.470
The problem, as you all know, is that coal
is a tremendously polluting source.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:40.410
And this not only impacts climate change,
but also the quality of life and health in

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.560
Chinese cities.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.330
Some days these cities literally seem to have
been built inside a fireplace.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:51.780
That’s precisely why the government is pushing
to replace this energy source with cleaner

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:55.290
alternatives: renewable energies, nuclear
energy, and natural gas.

00:04:55.290 --> 00:05:01.660
In other words, China's energy dependence
is and will be one of this country’s biggest

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:02.660
weaknesses.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:06.450
So, you can already see how important it is
for the Chinese government to control and

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:09.440
guarantee its oil and natural gas sources.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.540
If supply to these resources were impeded,
all the power and prosperity of the Asian

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:14.720
giant would collapse.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:23.000
When the Chinese government debates national
security, they have to talk about energy.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.340
And one other very important detail.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:29.030
China isn’t the only asian nation that depends
on energy supplies.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:33.639
Without delving too deep, two of its neighbors
and great rivals have even higher dependence

00:05:33.639 --> 00:05:34.639
levels.

00:05:34.639 --> 00:05:40.449
In Japan’s case, their dependence on Middle
Eastern oil exceeds 90% of its consumption

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:42.500
and in India’s case, 60%.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.450
Let me explain this in another way.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:48.870
Controlling hydrocarbon sources can not only
be useful to China from the security of supply

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:52.520
point of view, but also as a geopolitical
instrument.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:58.370
And of course, when all this depends largely
on a region riddled with so many problems,

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:02.669
well... let's just say that Beijing understood
it was high time to get a foothold in the

00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:03.669
Middle East.

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:07.050
Even more so if we consider that the country
with the most influence over this region right

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:11.039
now is its biggest rival: The United States.

00:06:11.039 --> 00:06:13.120
(CHART)

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.030
But that’s not the only reason.

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:22.960
There’s another reason that explains China’s
growing interest in the petro-monarchies region:

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.669
ensuring the safety of their trade routes.

00:06:25.669 --> 00:06:28.430
As you all know, the Chinese economy is very
focused on exports.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:33.260
Well, a substantial part of all Chinese trade
passes directly through the Middle East and,

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:37.919
in order for trade to flow, China depends
on both the ports and the so-called choke

00:06:37.919 --> 00:06:41.259
points in the area.

00:06:41.259 --> 00:06:45.770
These are critical points in many supply chains
and, if a rival such as the United States,

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:49.830
were able to close them, it would interrupt
most of China’s trade, both inbound and

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:50.830
outbound.

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:54.419
We’re talking about places such as the Suez
Canal, the Mandeb Strait or Hormuz Strait.

00:06:54.419 --> 00:07:00.050
And keep in mind that these are waters that
are full of American navy ships.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:03.860
Right now, this scenario doesn’t seem likely,
but it’s a risk that the Chinese authorities

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:05.040
are already considering.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:09.040
For these two reasons, the Middle East has
become a level one priority for the Chinese

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:10.040
government.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:13.470
But at this point, the question that pops
up is, how does the Chinese government want

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:19.199
to go about this, what is the Chinese government
doing to expand its influence in this region?

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:21.720
Any ideas?

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:24.240
Listen up.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:29.990
(CHINA’S NEW POWER PATHS)

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:34.610
In the field of international politics, the
strategy that the Chinese follow to achieve

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:38.880
their objectives is different from what we’re
used to.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.610
You see, China doesn’t want to gain control
in the Middle East by using its army, instead

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.880
it wants to use a very different mechanism.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:49.670
It’s nothing new, they’ve been doing it
all over the world.

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:54.250
The Asian giant wants to create a culture
of economic interdependence, one so strong

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:59.879
that it can give China enormous influence
among the region’s countries.

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:05.789
An interdependence that guarantees full access
to and, in some ways, control of all the oil

00:08:05.789 --> 00:08:07.150
and natural gas they need.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:11.720
This explains why China has positioned itself
as the largest investor in the region since

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:15.330
2017, that’s even above the United States
itself.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:20.220
One out of every three euros that are invested
in the Middle East come from Chinese money.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:25.610
It’s the only region in which Chinese foreign
investment continues to grow steadily.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:33.010
For example, in 2016 the Chinese company Sinopec
and the Saudi company Aramco agreed to jointly

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:37.260
build a refinery worth more than 10 billion
dollars in Saudi Arabia.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:41.640
In Kuwait, Sinopec also participated in the
construction of the largest refinery in the

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:49.360
world, a project worth more than 13 billion
dollars.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:50.360
And in Oman…

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:52.060
Quick question, have you ever heard of Duqm?

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:56.790
Well, keep this name in mind, because it’s
likely to become very well known soon.

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:02.350
Duqm is a new city and a new port that is
shooting up in a part of Oman where there

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:05.180
was hardly anything just five years ago.

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:08.780
Today it’s one of the largest special economic
zones in the entire Middle East.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:12.130
And it’s here that Chinese companies are
investing thousands and billions of dollars.

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:15.670
Refineries, ports, airports, hotels, offices,
business campuses...

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:19.930
We’re talking about a completely new industrial
city located at the gates of the Hormuz Strait.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:23.780
That is, Chinese shipping companies can operate
without having to enter the Persian Gulf.

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:25.940
And it’s not only a haven for shipping companies.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:33.410
Among the billion-dollar Chinese investments,
there are factories of all kinds.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:38.140
These are only three examples of the huge
investments China is pouring into this area

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:39.140
of ​​the world.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:43.580
Back in 2008, the region barely received 1%
of all the money that China invested globally,

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:47.290
today there is Chinese money all over the
Middle East.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:52.080
But the Chinese don’t only invest their
money, they also have no problem selling all

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:58.000
kinds of technology to these countries, especially
military technology.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.610
For example, many of the military drones and
ballistic missiles that are operated in the

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:02.970
region are Chinese-made.

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:07.290
And all this comes at precisely the same time
as the other great power, the American Empire,

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.620
is radically changing its strategy for the
Middle East.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:15.620
Listen up.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:21.130
(THE ESCAPE ROUTE)

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:25.730
After investing hundreds of billions of dollars
and paying a huge price in human lives, the

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:28.500
United States seems to be done with the Middle
East.

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:35.220
Especially now that they’re about to achieve
full energy independence.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:40.730
Between 2004 and 2007 the United States imported
about 12 million barrels per day but this

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:44.300
figure had decreased to just over 2 million
by 2017.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:46.529
That is, oil imports have decreased by 80%.

00:10:46.529 --> 00:10:50.740
What’s more, according to the International
Energy Agency, it’ll only be a matter of

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:54.600
a few years until the United States becomes
a net exporter of oil and natural gas.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:59.110
It’s set to become quite the competitor
for the Gulf petro-monarchies.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:00.910
(CHART)

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:07.329
Well, the situation is that the Obama Administration
had already announced its intention to decrease

00:11:07.329 --> 00:11:12.100
the United States’ presence in the Middle
East, and to prioritize the Asia Pacific region.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:16.709
Meanwhile, one of Donald Trump’s great promises
ran along the same lines.

00:11:16.709 --> 00:11:25.060
(AUDIO: Does the USA want to be the Policeman
of the Middle East, getting NOTHING but spending

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:30.810
precious lives and trillions of dollars protecting
others who, in almost all cases, do not appreciate

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:31.810
what we are doing?

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:33.610
Do we want to be there forever?

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:36.180
Time for others to finally fight.

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.210
DONALD TRUMP)
https://twitter.com/realDonaldTrump/status/1075721703421042688

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:42.079
To achieve this, the Trump Administration
has a plan: to delegate power, to leave the

00:11:42.079 --> 00:11:46.700
responsibility in the hands of two close allies,
a military leader and a political leader.

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:54.470
I’m talking about Israel and Saudi Arabia.

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:58.390
That explains why, since Trump arrived at
the White House, his relations with Ryad and

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:00.529
Jerusalem have become very, very good.

00:12:00.529 --> 00:12:05.680
Saudi Arabia has been willing to allow it
and to forgive everything.

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:09.270
A blockade against the country where the United
States has its largest military base in the

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:10.270
region?

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:11.270
Go ahead.

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:13.940
(AUDIO: Donald Trump tweets support for blockade
imposed on Qatar.

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:14.940
The Guardian)

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.480
Congress wants to veto the sale of weapons
to Saudi Arabia so they won’t use them in

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:18.480
the Yemen War?

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:19.480
None of that.

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:23.110
(AUDIO: Donald Trump vetoes bills prohibiting
arms sales to Saudi Arabia.

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:24.110
The Guardian)

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:26.490
The Saudi authorities killed a journalist
who worked for the Washington Post...

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:29.290
Ummm alright let's just look the other way.

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:33.399
(AUDIO: Trump defends Saudi Arabia ties despite
Khashoggi murder.

00:12:33.399 --> 00:12:34.399
BBC)

00:12:34.399 --> 00:12:36.190
Ryad is asking you to take care of their air
defense?

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:37.190
No problem!

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:42.200
(AUDIO: US to send more forces to Saudi Arabia
despite Trump’s pledge to reduce Middle

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:43.950
East presence.

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:45.649
CNBC)

00:12:45.649 --> 00:12:48.910
But the question is, why?

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:52.350
Because it would be a great political success
for Trump to get out of the Middle East and

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:57.370
to foster a peace agreement between Israel
and Palestine... which all American presidents

00:12:57.370 --> 00:12:59.640
since Jimmy Carter, have tried to do.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:02.700
But wait a minute, what does Palestine have
to do with all this?

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:06.260
Well, it looks like the Trump Administration
wants to present a peace agreement before

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:07.270
the next elections.

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:11.710
A peace agreement that, for the first time,
could be backed by the neighbouring Arab countries,

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:14.720
which would force the Palestinian Authorities
to accept it.

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:19.240
That, at least, is Trump’s idea.

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:23.779
Anyway, as you can see, some want to hop off
and others want to hop on.

00:13:23.779 --> 00:13:27.990
Two different models to control one of the
most strategically important regions on the

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:28.990
planet.

00:13:28.990 --> 00:13:29.990
The questions remaining are:

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:32.079
Will Trump's model to control the Middle East
work?

00:13:32.079 --> 00:13:35.889
Or will we end up being locked out of an emerging
Chinese domain?

00:13:35.889 --> 00:13:39.360
Will all these actions from the big powers
be good for the region’s stability or will

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:42.389
they only add fuel to the fire of old conflicts?

00:13:42.389 --> 00:13:44.339
Leave your answer in the comments.

